    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Дела хорошо пошли?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Business is going well?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'let's go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Много работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A lot of work.' Timur combines 'a lot' with 'of work' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я взял племянника.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hired my nephew.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'nephew'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Его зовут Суслан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'His name is Suslan.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'him' with 'Suslan'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что ты привёз?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did you bring?' Chef Viktor links 'what' directly with 'you' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Суслан, разгружай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Suslan, unload.' Timur combines 'Suslan' with 'unload' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "suslan",
      "t": "Я нанимался водителем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I was hired as a driver.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'sign up for', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "suslan",
      "t": "А не грузчиком.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not as a loader.' Notice how 'and' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'mover'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот скотина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a bastard.' Notice how 'there' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'brute'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот ваша рыба, шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is your fish, Chef.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'there' with 'chef'. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Зачем мне рыба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do I need fish?' Chef Viktor links 'what for' directly with 'to me' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Увози обратно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Take it back.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'take away' straight to 'back'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
